%[text] ## Fit Polynomial Regression Model
%[text] ### Generate Polynomial Data
%[text] In the code block below, a random number generator is used to create a dataset that follows a third‑order polynomial. The generated data are stored in a table, `T`, with variables labeled `x` and `y`. You can use [dot notation](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/creating_plots/access-and-modify-property-values.html) to extract the variables x and y from the table. 
%[text] ![](text:image:13bb) **Try**. Click **Generate Data** to generate a synthetic polynomial data set and store it in a table, `T,` with variables x and `y.`
  %[control:button:010b]{"position":[1,2]}
T = GenData; % Use function to generate data
head(T) % View top rows of table data and the column labels
x = T.x; % Extract the input variable from the table
y = T.y; % Extract the response variable from the table
%%
%[text] ### Plot the Data
%[text] ![](text:image:48af) **Try**. Make a [scatter](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/scatter.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_scatter) plot of the data by clicking **Plot the Data**.
% Plot original response and fitted polynomial
scatter(x,y,"filled",DisplayName="Data",SeriesIndex=3) % Make a scatter plot of the data
legend("show",Location="Best") % Add a descriptive legend
xlabel("x") % Label x-axis
ylabel("y") % Label y-axis
title("Polynomial Data") % Add a descriptive title
  %[control:button:4286]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Find and View the Best-Fit for the Polynomial Data
%[text] ![](text:image:3aff) **Try**. We can use the [`polyfit`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/polyfit.html) function in MATLAB to calculate the coefficients ($a\_0${"altText":"a\_0"} $a\_1${"altText":"a\_1"}, $\\dots${"altText":"\dots"}, $a\_p${"altText":"a\_p"}) for a regression line, and we can determine the values of the fit using the [`polyval`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/polyval.html) function then click **Evaluate the Polynomial Fit** to calculate the coefficients and evaluate the values of the polynomial function.
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:515e) **Tip**. Include [`hold on` ](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/hold.html#d126e768349)at the end of your code to keep the existing plot on the same axes for plotting the predicted values in the next section. 
% Determine 3rd-order polynomial regression model using polyfit
a = polyfit(x,y,3); % 'a' contains coefficients [a3 a2 a1 a0]
% Predict responses from the fitted model
yfit = polyval(a,x);
% Plot fitted polynomial vs. x on top of existing scatter plot
hold on % Retain current plot on axes
plot(x,yfit,LineWidth=1.5,DisplayName="Polynomial Fit",SeriesIndex="none")
legend("show",Location="Best")
box on
hold on % Retain the current plot on axes
  %[control:button:9b0e]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ## Polynomial Model Prediction & Visualization
%[text] ### Use a Polynomial Regression Model to Predict One Value
%[text] We can use the [`polyval`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/polyval.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_polyval) function in MATLAB to predict specific values of the polynomial regression model. In the example code block below, we demonstrate the use of the [`polyval`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/polyval.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_polyval) function.
%[text] ![](text:image:3453) **Try**. We use [`polyval`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/polyval.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_polyval) to predict the value for 0.67 and use `scatter` to plot the predicted value marked by a size 60 filled diamond. Set the `DisplayName` in the call to `scatter` for use in the legend then click **Plot the Predicted Value on the Curve**.
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:2ba8) **Tip**. Run the previous section to load the data and create the linear regression model, and include [`hold off`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/matlab/ref/hold.html?searchPort=60962#d126e778590) after plotting the predicted values to stop retaining the current plot and restore the default axes.
PredictedValue = polyval(a,0.67)
scatter(0.67,PredictedValue,60,"d","filled",DisplayName="Prediction")
hold off % Release all plots from axes
  %[control:button:107c]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Use a Polynomial Regression Model to Predict Multiple Values
%[text] ![](text:image:33bd) **Try**. We can even use [`polyval`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/polyval.html?s_tid=srchtitle_support_results_1_polyval) to predict the responses for a range of values. Click **Predict Multiple Values** to predict the responses for x-values of -1.6, -0.53, 0.38, and 1.9.
xNew = [-1.6;-0.53;0.38;1.9]; % points to predict
yPredictions = polyval(a,xNew); % evaluate fitted polynomial at these points
disp(table(xNew,yPredictions,VariableNames={'x','yPred'}))
  %[control:button:649e]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ### Plot Multiple Predicted Values on Best-Fit Plot
