%[text] ## ![](text:image:21aa) **Application 1. Centrifugal Pump**
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Illustration of a centrifugal pump](text:image:5714)
%[text] Centrifugal pumps are commonly used in chemical process plants to transport liquids through piping networks connecting unit operations. At a fixed rotational speed, the relationship between **pump head** and **volumetric flow rate** is described by the pump characteristic curve, which is often approximated by a **quadratic polynomial** over the normal operating range:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $H(Q)=a+bQ+cQ^2${"altText":"H(Q)=a+bQ+cQ^2"} 
%[text] where $H${"altText":"H"} is the pump head (m) and Q is the flow rate $(m^3/s)${"altText":"(m^3\/s)"}.
